<!-- ID: detailed_analysis -->
Evidence and findings:
- `src/scribe_mcp/doc_management/quality/scopes.py` now models `inline_code` and `indented_code` scopes in addition to fenced code, so the shared document context can classify the exact example forms that previously leaked through.
- `src/scribe_mcp/doc_management/scaffold_quality.py` still preserves the existing warning policy table; `SCF_PLACEHOLDER_BRACKET` remains `critical` and `blocking=True`. The suppression helper now consults `DocumentContext` for `fenced_code`, `inline_code`, and `indented_code`, and both placeholder and lifecycle checks route through that helper.
- `tests/test_manage_docs_scaffold_quality.py` now includes focused regressions for nested fences, inline-code placeholder examples, indented-code placeholder examples, and table-row bracket text. The earlier authoritative payload-shape test remains in place.
- `tests/test_manage_docs_quality_check.py` continues to assert the facade response contains the structured keys expected by callers, including warning metadata and top-level proof fields.
- Package scope remains bounded on the implementation side: this fix changes scaffold-quality behavior only. Research/changelog evaluators were not migrated or broadened by the protected-scope repair. There are unrelated pre-existing dirty-worktree changes in `.scribe/docs/GLOBAL_CHANGELOG.md`, `src/scribe_mcp/doc_management/runtime.py`, and `tests/test_manage_docs_quality_check.py`; they should not be mistaken for Package 2.2 implementation scope.
- CHECKLIST item `p2-scaffold-rules` is still materially truthful, but its proof text is slightly stale because it still cites `scaffold_quality 20 passed` and mentions only nested-fence/table regressions. A small follow-up wording refresh would make it fully current by noting `22 passed` and the new inline/indented protected-scope regressions.
<!-- ID: recommendations -->
Recommendations:
- Route Package 2.3 now that Package 2.2 has a package-specific PASS.
- Refresh CHECKLIST proof text for `p2-scaffold-rules` so it reflects the new inline-code and indented-code regressions and the updated `22 passed` scaffold-quality count.
- Keep the protected-scope probes in mind for future scaffold-rule extractions; the contract is broader than fenced blocks alone.
